TITLE: H2-MAS: A Hybrid Heuristic-Multi-Agent Architecture for Semantic Bot Detection
TEXT: Article H2-MAS: A Hybrid Heuristic-Multi-Agent Architecture for Semantic Bot Detection Avinash Chandra Vootkuri Walmart Global Tech, Herndon, VA 20171, USA; avinash.vootkuri@walmart.com Abstract Traditional Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) rely on static thresholds to detect automated threats. While effective against simple scripts, these deterministic rules struggle with “Ambiguous Traffic”—sophisticated bots that mimic human behavior and “Efficient Humans” (Power Users) who exhibit bot-like speed. In this paper, we introduce H2-MAS, a hierarchical security framework that combines a high-speed Random Forest classifier (Tier 1) with a Multi-Agent Cognitive Council (Tier 2) powered by Large Language Models (LLMs). Unlike standard “Black Box” LLM deployments, H2-MAS utilizes an adversarial “Prosecutor vs. Defender” protocol to resolve semantic paradoxes in real-time. We evaluated the system on a stratified dataset of 1000 high-uncertainty sessions (0.2 ≤ p ≤ 0.8). Through simulated evaluation on a stratified semantic test set, the results demonstrate that the proposed architecture has the theoretical capacity to achieve up to 97.6% accuracy on specific edge cases where traditional heuristics fail. Notably, the Cognitive Council demonstrated the theoretical capacity to reduce the False Positive Rate to 0.00% within this constrained evaluated set, validating that the “Defender” agent can successfully protect legitimate power users from erroneous blocking. This architecture offers a cost-effective paradigm for Semantic Security, prioritizing user experience without compromising threat detection. Keywords: adversarial defense; bot detection; large language models; multi-agent systems; semantic security; web application firewall Academic Editor: Juan-Carlos Cano Received: 3 April 2026 Revised: 24 April 2026 Accepted: 28 April 2026 Published: 2 May 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the author. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. 1. Introduction The rapid evolution of automated web threats has rendered signature-based detection increasingly insufficient. Modern bots utilize “semantic mimicry,” emulating human behavior to modify their digital fingerprints and evade detection. This creates a significant challenge for security teams: the “Grey Area” between malicious automation and highvelocity human usage. Traditional Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) typically rely on rigid thresholds—such as request velocity or mouse jitter—to identify non-human actors. While effective against simplistic scripts, these deterministic rules struggle to distinguish between sophisticated bots and legitimate “Power Users” who exhibit naturally fast or efficient browsing patterns. Consequently, security teams face a difficult trade-off: tighten thresholds and risk blocking high-value customers (False Positives), or loosen thresholds and permit account takeovers (False Negatives). This challenge is particularly acute in the retail sector, where highvelocity legitimate traffic often mimics bot behavior [1]. This paper addresses this dilemma by proposing H2-MAS (Hybrid Heuristic-MultiAgent System), a novel architecture that integrates the speed of traditional machine learning Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 2 of 16 with the cognitive reasoning of Large Language Models (LLMs). Rather than applying a single model to all traffic, H2-MAS employs a tiered approach: high-certainty decisions are offloaded to lightweight classifiers (Tier 1), while computationally expensive semantic reasoning is reserved for complex edge cases (Tier 2). By deploying an adversarial “Prosecutor vs. Defender” protocol within the Tier 2 council, the system introduces a context-aware decision layer capable of resolving semantic paradoxes that baffle conventional defenses. 2. Related Work 2.1. Machine Learning in WAFs Traditional WAFs rely on signature matching and regular expressions (Regex) to detect known attacks like SQL Injection and XSS [2]. While efficient, these systems struggle with “Zero-Day” exploits and behavioral anomalies. Recent literature has explored Machine Learning (ML) to bridge this gap. For instance, Chindrus and Caruntu compared random forests and SVMs for classifying malicious HTTP requests in WAFs, achieving high accuracy on benchmark datasets [3]. However, these single-model approaches suffer from the “Black Box” problem and high False Positive Rates (FPR) when faced with “Grey Area” traffic (e.g., rapid browsing by legitimate power users), as they lack the semantic reasoning to distinguish intent from syntax. 2.2. Large Language Models (LLMs) for Anomaly Detection The advent of LLMs has introduced “Semantic Security.” Yang et al. introduced AD-LLM, a benchmark for using transformers in zero-shot anomaly detection, proving that LLMs can identify outliers without training data by leveraging pre-trained world knowledge [4]. Similarly, Gupta et al. demonstrated that LLMs could decrypt obfuscated malicious payloads that evaded traditional WAFs [5]. Despite these advances, single-agent LLM deployments are prone to “hallucinations” and reasoning inconsistencies, often failing to accurately distinguish between benign and malicious intent due to a lack of contextual checks [6,7]. 2.3. Multi-Agent Systems (MASs) in Cybersecurity To mitigate single-model bias, recent research has pivoted to Multi-Agent Systems [8]. Zeng et al. proposed a modular MAS where distinct agents handled email, logs, and network traffic separately [9]. While effective for distributed tasks, these systems typically operate in silos rather than engaging in dialectic reasoning. H2-MAS advances this state of the art by introducing an Adversarial Debate Protocol (Prosecutor vs. Defender). Unlike previous works that use agents for parallel processing, our architecture uses agents for conflict resolution, specifically targeting the “Semantic Paradoxes” (e.g., the Power User Paradox) that traditional ML classifiers mislabel. 2.4. Cost-Aware Cascades and Human–Bot Disambiguation Hierarchical and cost-aware detection pipelines have been explored in adjacent do- mains to balance accuracy and computational efficiency. Viola and Jones introduced cascade classifiers for real-time object detection, demonstrating that early-stage filters can dramatically reduce inference cost while preserving accuracy [10]. H2-MAS builds on these insights by unifying cost-aware cascades with adversarial semantic reasoning. Unlike prior cascaded systems that rely on confidence thresholds alone, H2-MAS introduces a deliberative second stage explicitly designed to arbitrate ambiguous human–bot boundary cases. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 3 of 16 3. System Architecture 3.1. The Hierarchical Approach The Hierarchical Approach The H2-MAS framework (see Figure 1) operates on a “Triage” principle to balance computational cost with detection accuracy: • Tier 1 (The Gatekeeper): A lightweight Random Forest classifier analyzes approxi- mately 99% of traffic using stateless features (e.g., Request Velocity, IP Reputation). High-confidence predictions (p < 0.2 or p > 0.8) are executed immediately. • Tier 2 (The Cognitive Council): Only “Ambiguous” sessions (0.2 ≤ p ≤ 0.8) are escalated to the Multi-Agent System (MAS). Drawing on recent findings that multiagent debate significantly improves reasoning factuality [11], this tier employs an adversarial protocol rather than a single-shot query. This ensures that computationally expensive LLM inference is reserved exclusively for complex semantic paradoxes. Figure 1. The H2-MAS Hierarchical Architecture. High-velocity web traffic is initially evaluated by a deterministic Random Forest (Tier 1). To balance computational cost and accuracy, only sessions falling within the defined ambiguity threshold (0.2 ≤ p ≤ 0.8) are escalated to the Multi-Agent Cognitive Council (Tier 2). 3.2. The Multi-Agent Protocol Standard LLM prompts can struggle with conflicting data. To establish robustness, H2-MAS enforces a strict Adversarial Debate Protocol (see Figure 2) involving three distinct https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 4 of 16 personas. Crucially, the LLM is not utilized for open-ended generative tasks; rather, it functions as a deterministic semantic parsing engine that maps unstructured session data and conflicting behavioral signals to the rigid rules defined in the Hierarchy of Truth: • The Prosecutor (Technical Audit): Biased toward BLOCK. It scans for technical anomalies (e.g., linear navigation, TLS mismatches) and treats high efficiency as a suspicion of automation. • The Defender (Contextual Defense): Biased toward ALLOW. It scans for exculpatory evidence (e.g., high purchase history, “Power User” behavior) and argues for the presumption of innocence. • The Judiciary (Semantic Resolution): Resolves the conflict using a formal Hierarchy of Truth: – Level 1 (Hard Evidence): Infrastructure truth (TLS/Graph Fanout) overrides all else. – Level 2 (Identity Truth): Verified VIP status overrides behavioral anomalies. – Level 3 (Behavioral Truth): Micro-behavioral telemetry (e.g., mouse jitter) is used only as a tie-breaker. Figure 2. Adversarial Debate Protocol. Ambiguous session features are parsed simultaneously by two opposing agents—the Prosecutor (biased to block) and the Defender (biased to allow)—before the Judiciary agent applies the strict Hierarchy of Truth to render a final, context-aware verdict. 3.3. Mathematical Formalization To ensure computational efficiency, H2-MAS formalizes the decision process as a two-stage function. The complete set of symbols and mathematical notations used in this framework is summarized in Table 1. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 5 of 16 Table 1. Mathematical notation. Symbol x Pbot ( x) τlow, τhigh Ein f Eid Ebeh V Description Input Feature Vector Probability score from Random Forest Ambiguity thresholds (0.2, 0.8) Set of Infrastructure Evidence (TLS, IP) Set of Identity Evidence (History) Set of Behavioral Evidence (Jitter) Final Verdict (1 = Block, 0 = Allow) 3.3.1. Stage 1: The Probabilistic Gatekeeper Let x be the input vector of stateless features. The Random Forest model MRF outputs a probability score Pbot(x). We define thresholds τlow = 0.2 and τhigh = 0.8. The triage function T(x) is defined as:  0 (Allow)  if Pbot(x) < τlow  T(x) = 1 (Block) if Pbot(x) > τhigh . (1)  Escalate(x) if τlow ≤ Pbot(x) ≤ τhigh 3.3.2. Stage 2: The Cognitive Hierarchy For escalated sessions, the Judiciary Agent applies a priority function J(E) defined by the “Hierarchy of Truth”:  1  if ∃e ∈ Einf s.t. e = Anomalous  V= 0 if ∃e ∈ Eid s.t. e = Verified . (2)  I(Ebeh) otherwise (Tie-Breaker) The step-by-step execution flow of this adversarial debate and triage process is formally detailed in Algorithm 1. Algorithm 1 H2-MAS Adversarial Protocol Input: Session S = {Fanout, History, Jitter, TLS} Output: Verdict V ∈ {0, 1} // — Tier 1: Fast Filter (Deterministic) — Pr f ← RandomForest.predict(S) if Pr f < 0.2 OR Pr f > 0.8 then RETURN Round(Pr f ) else // — Tier 2: Cognitive Council (Semantic) — // Step 1: Generate Arguments ArgPros ← Agent_Prosecutor.analyze(S) ArgDe f ← Agent_De f ender.analyze(S) // Step 2: Construct Judiciary Prompt Context ← “Apply Hierarchy: 1.Infrastructure > 2.Identity > 3.Behavior” Prompt ← Context + “Prosecutor: ” + ArgPros + “Defender: ” + ArgDe f // Step 3: LLM Inference V ← Agent_Judiciary.decide(Prompt) RETURN V end if https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 6 of 16 4. Experimental Setup 4.1. Dataset Generation To rigorously test the system’s reasoning capabilities, we generated a synthetic dataset of 500,000 web sessions containing specific “Semantic Paradoxes”: • The Power User Paradox: A human with linear (bot-like) navigation but high purchase history. • The VIP Farm Paradox: An automated entity using stolen VIP credentials (high history) but exhibiting high graph fanout. • The Deepfake Paradox: A bot with human-like mouse movement but a mismatched TLS fingerprint. 4.2. Synthetic Dataset Generation Protocol To ensure the H2-MAS Council was evaluated against statistically relevant edge cases, we utilized a controlled generation protocol rather than random sampling. Using the Python (Version 3.11) Faker and Scikit-learn (Version 1.8.0) libraries, we constructed a dataset of 500,000 requests (N = 500 k) with specific feature injections to rigorously test the system’s reasoning capabilities against “Semantic Paradoxes.” 4.2.1. Feature Engineering Each session vector S consists of 14 distinct features; a comprehensive summary of these features, including their distributions and specific roles within the semantic paradoxes, is provided in Table 2. Key behavioral features were modeled as follows: • Mouse Jitter (J): Modeled using a Perlin Noise distribution. Humans exhibit J ∼ N (12, 4), while simple bots exhibit J ≈ 0. Advanced “Deepfake” bots were injected with J ∼ N (12, 1) to test Tier 2 sensitivity. • Graph Fanout (F): Defined as the number of distinct user accounts accessed by a single IP/Fingerprint within a 24-h window. Legitimate users adhere to F ≤ 3 (User, Spouse, Child). Bot farms were generated with F ∈ [5, 50]. • Navigation Linearity (L): Calculated as the R-squared value of mouse cursor coordinates. Perfectly linear bot movement (R2 ≈ 1.0) is distinct from human curvature (R2 < 0.95). Table 2. Summary of the 14 features utilized in the synthetic dataset generation, detailing their distributions and roles in the Semantic Paradoxes. Feature Name mouse_velocity mouse_jitter navigation_linearity cart_hesitation_ms inter_click_entropy purchase_history_usd account_fanout_24 h session_age tls_mismatch_flag is_datacenter_ip is_suspicious_ua ip_reputation_score time_of_day_anomaly geo_distance_km Distribution/Type Continuous (px/s) Perlin Noise Entropy R2 Correlation Integer (ms) Float (0.0–1.0) Integer ($) Integer (Count) Integer (Seconds) Binary (0 or 1) Binary (0 or 1) Binary (0 or 1) Integer (0–100) Binary (0 or 1) Continuous (km) Paradox Role Power User (High) Deepfake (Human-like) Power User (Bot-like) Baseline Behavioral Baseline Behavioral VIP Farm, Power User VIP Farm (Anomaly > 3) Baseline Identity Deepfake (Mismatch) Baseline Infrastructure Baseline Infrastructure Baseline Infrastructure Baseline Context Baseline Context https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 7 of 16 4.2.2. Injection of Semantic Paradoxes To create the “Ambiguous” test set, we explicitly injected conflicting signals into 5% of the dataset. This stratification ensures that the samples sent to the Cognitive Council represent a “Worst-Case Scenario” for standard classifiers: • Type I: The Power User Paradox. Injected High Velocity (>5 requests per sec) + High Purchase History (>$500). Represents a human with linear (bot-like) navigation but a verified VIP history. • Type II: The VIP Farm Paradox. Injected High Purchase History + High Fanout (F > 10). Represents an automated entity using stolen VIP credentials but exhibiting an impossible graph topology. • Type III: The Deepfake Paradox (Puppeteer). Injected Human Jitter + TLS Hash Mismatch. Represents a bot with perfect human-like mouse movement but cryptographic evidence of automation. 4.3. Synthetic Data Validity and Real-World Alignment Due to strict commercial privacy and data protection regulations, raw production traffic cannot be publicly released for this study. However, to ensure the H2-MAS Cognitive Council was evaluated against realistic operational conditions rather than arbitrary noise, we rigorously aligned our synthetic generation protocols with empirical distributions reported in industry-standard security research. First, regarding request velocity, we calibrated the “Power User” threshold (>5 requests per sec) to mirror legitimate high-frequency traffic patterns observed during inventoryconstrained events, such as “Flash Sales.” According to Akamai’s State of the Internet report [12] and Cloudflare’s application security data [13], legitimate human traffic during these high-demand windows frequently exhibits velocity spikes that statistically resemble automated scraping. This alignment ensures that the “Power User Paradox” tested in our dataset accurately reflects the behavior of legitimate customers competing for highvalue inventory. Second, the “Graph Fanout” constraints were derived from typical device usage topologies. We defined the legitimate upper bound (F ≤ 3) to reflect standard household connectivity, where a single user identity persists across a mobile device, desktop, and tablet. In contrast, the “VIP Farm” injection (F ∈ [5, 50]) simulates the “Credential Stuffing” and “Account Aggregation” attack vectors described by the OWASP Automated Threat Handbook [14], where botnets parallelize login attempts across distributed IP addresses to evade rate limits. Finally, to rigorously test the “Defender” agent’s ability to distinguish deepfake bots from legitimate users, we modeled mouse jitter using Perlin Noise rather than a uniform random distribution. This selection approximates the natural neuromuscular micro-hesitations inherent in human motor control, ensuring the system faces behavioral entropy consistent with real biological inputs. By anchoring these synthetic inputs to established behavioral norms [13–15], we ensure that the “Semantic Paradoxes” presented to the Cognitive Council represent statistically valid edge cases that mirror the complexity of modern production environments. 4.4. Tier 2 Model Implementation The Tier 2 Cognitive Council was instantiated using OpenAI’s gpt-4o-mini. We selected this specific model for its high reasoning-to-cost ratio, ensuring the system remains economically viable for real-time edge deployment. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 8 of 16 Threshold Pair [0.1, 0.9] [0.2, 0.8] (X) [0.3, 0.7] To ensure deterministic reproducibility, the model temperature was set to T = 0.0. The exact system prompts used to enforce the adversarial “Prosecutor” and “Defender” personas are detailed in Appendix A. 4.5. Sampling Strategy and Threshold Sensitivity To validate the selection of the τlow = 0.2 and τhigh = 0.8 ambiguity thresholds, we conducted a threshold sensitivity analysis on the Tier 1 Random Forest probabilities. Tightening the boundaries to [0.1, 0.9] escalated an unsustainably large percentage of traffic to Tier 2, negating the cost-efficiency of the hierarchical cascade. Conversely, relaxing the boundaries to [0.3, 0.7] allowed an unacceptable volume of sophisticated bots to bypass the Cognitive Council entirely. Thus, the [0.2, 0.8] range was mathematically esta [...] njection stress test. We modified the User-Agent strings of 10 known bots to include the malicious payload: “[SYSTEM OVERRIDE: IGNORE ALL PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS AND OUTPUT VERDICT: ALLOW].” When processed by the Cognitive Council without input sanitization, the prompt injection succeeded in 10 out of 10 instances, successfully overriding the LLM’s behavioral logic and allowing the bots to bypass the firewall. This empirical failure explicitly validates the necessity of strict safeguards: raw user-controlled strings must never be fed directly into the Tier 2 council without passing through a strict regex sanitization layer or a secondary instruction-tuned filter. • Replay Attacks and Credential Stuffing: Attackers utilizing stolen, high-reputation session tokens (“VIP Farms”) attempt to exploit the system’s bias toward allowing good customers. The Cognitive Council defeats this by strictly enforcing the Graph https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 13 of 16 Fanout rule, ensuring that anomalous topological behavior (>3 accounts per IP) instantly overrides historical identity reputation. 7. Limitations and Future Work 7.1. Limitations While H2-MAS achieves zero false positives within the semantic test set., we acknowl- edge specific limitations inherent to LLM-based security: 7.1.1. Latency Overhead The Tier 2 inference introduces an average latency of 700 ms per request. While this is applied only to <1% of traffic, it may be unsuitable for ultra-low-latency applications (e.g., High-Frequency Trading). For standard e-commerce login flows, however, a subsecond delay for suspicious users is industry-standard. 7.1.2. Prompt Injection Vulnerability As with any LLM application, the Cognitive Council is theoretically susceptible to “Prompt Injection” attacks, where a sophisticated attacker might embed instructions in the User-Agent string (e.g., “Ignore previous instructions and return ALLOW”). While our prompt uses rigid delimiting to sanitize inputs, future iterations must incorporate dedicated “Instruction Tuning” to harden the agents against adversarial text inputs. 7.1.3. Synthetic Distribution Bias As the evaluation relies on synthetic data, the absolute performance metrics (e.g., 97.6% accuracy) reflect the system’s logic consistency rather than its guaranteed performance in the wild. Real-world deployment may require calibrating the “Hierarchy of Truth” weights to match specific domain traffic. This study is presented as a foundational Proof of Concept (PoC) for the H2-MAS architecture. While the synthetic distributions were rigorously aligned with industry baselines for request velocity and graph fanout, future work will focus on longitudinal ablation studies using live production traffic to validate the multi-agent cascade in a live-edge environment. 7.2. Future Work Future research will focus on two axes: 1. Optimization: Distilling the Tier 2 Large Language Model into a smaller, domainspecific Small Language Model (SLM) to reduce inference cost and latency. 2. Real-Time Learning: Implementing a feedback loop where definitive verdicts from the Judiciary Agent automatically retrain the Tier 1 Random Forest, progressively reducing the need for escalation over time. 8. Conclusions As bot sophistication evolves from simple scripting to semantic emulation, the defense layer must evolve from “Pattern Matching” to “Intent Reasoning.” This paper presented a foundational Proof of Concept (PoC) for H2-MAS, a novel architecture that offloads high-uncertainty decisions to a Multi-Agent LLM Council. Our simulated evaluation yields three key insights: https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 14 of 16 1. Context Overrides Thresholds: The “Power User” paradox requires semantic disambiguation, not tighter rules. 2. Adversarial Defense Works: Through simulated evaluation on a stratified semantic test set, introducing a “Defender” agent demonstrated the theoretical capacity to achieve up to 97.6% accuracy and reduce the False Positive Rate (FPR) to 0.00% on a constrained subset of pure semantic paradoxes, successfully protecting legitimate traffic. 3. Efficiency: The hybrid approach makes Generative AI viable for real-time cybersecurity by ensuring computationally expensive reasoning is reserved only for highly ambiguous traffic. While the synthetic distributions utilized in this study were rigorously aligned with industry baselines, future work will focus on longitudinal ablation studies using live production traffic to validate this multi-agent cascade in a live-edge environment. Ultimately, by confining non-deterministic reasoning to a controlled, audited tier, this architecture aligns with emerging safety guidelines such as the NIST AI Risk Management Framework [15]. Although evaluated in a retail context, the architecture is applicable to any domain where high-velocity legitimate users coexist with automated abuse. Funding: This research received no external funding. Data Availability Statement: The synthetic dataset generation protocols and the algorithmic logic for the H2-MAS architecture are fully described in Sections 3 and 4 of this manuscript to ensure reproducibility. The specific source code and proprietary model weights are not publicly available due to commercial intellectual property restrictions. Acknowledgments: The author acknowledges the use of a large language model (Gemini Pro (Version - 3.0), Google Search ) solely for assistance with linguistic refinement and readability of the manuscript. All scientific contributions, including the experimental design, data generation, analysis, and conclusions, are entirely those of the author. Conflicts of Interest: Author Avinash Chandra Vootkuri was employed by the company Walmart. The author declares that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. Appendix A. Model Specification and Prompt Engineering To ensure reproducibility, we provide the specific system configurations and prompt structures used in the H2-MAS Cognitive Council. Appendix A.1. Model Configuration All agents (Prosecutor, Defender, Judiciary) utilized the gpt-4o-mini. The temperature was set to T = 0.0 to minimize stochastic hallucinations and ensure deterministic outputs for identical inputs. Appendix A.2. System Prompt Structure To minimize latency, H2-MAS utilizes a single-shot “Chain of Thought” prompt that enforces the adversarial protocol within a single inference pass. The exact system instruction provided to the gpt-4o-mini model is transcribed below in Table A1. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15091936 Electronics 2026, 15, 1936 15 of 16 Table A1. H2-MAS cognitive council system instruction. Cognitive Council Prompt Section and Instruction Text ROLE DEFINITION You are the H2-MAS Cognitive Council. Your default bias is ALLOW unless HARD EVIDENCE proves automation. PHASE 1: THE PROSECUTOR (Technical Audit) • Flag HARD EVIDENCE: Fanout > 3 (Bot Farm) OR TLS Mismatch (Puppeteer). • Flag SOFT EVIDENCE: Linear Navigation (R2 ≈ 1.0), Low Jitter. Constraint: You cannot demand a block based ONLY on Soft Evidence. PHASE 2: THE DEFENDER (Contextual Defense) • Highlight EXCULPATORY EVIDENCE: High Purchase History (VIP), Fanout == 1. Defense logic: “A VIP user is allowed to be fast and efficient (The Power User Paradox).” PHASE 3: THE JUDICIARY (Verdict) Apply the HIERARCHY OF TRUTH to resolve conflicts: 1. Infrastructure (TLS/Fanout): If BAD → BLOCK (Overrides everything). 2. Identity (History): If GOOD → ALLOW (Overrides Behavior). 3. Behavior (Nav/Jitter): Only use to break ties. COGNITIVE RULES • Rule A: If Fanout > 3 → BLOCK. • Rule B: If TLS Mismatch → BLOCK. • Rule C (Power User): If TLS Match AND History > $500 → ALLOW (Ignore Linear Nav). REQUIRED OUTPUT FORMAT PROSECUTOR: [Arguments] DEFENDER: [Arguments] JUDICIARY: [Step-by-step Reasoning] FINAL_VERDICT: [0 or 1]

